{{Politics of Balisca}}
In Balisca the term '''commune district''' ({{lang-bl|distrito comuna}}) refers to an {{W|electoral district}} or constituency in which the country is divided for the purpose of determining the delegates of the [[Confederal Congress]]. Confederal commune districts are contiguous and compact territories composed of adjacent local government units where practicable. They are {{W|single-member district}}s which return one member each to Congress, elected to serve a maximum of five consecutive three-year terms (15-years) through an {{W|instant-runoff voting}} system.  

The [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS) conducts a constitutionally mandated [[Census in Balisca|quinquennial census]] whose figures are used to determine the number of congressional districts to which each region, canton, or metropolis is entitled, in a process called [[w:Apportionment (politics)|apportionment]]. As a result of the [[2025 Baliscan electoral redistribution|2025 apportionment]] (using [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]] data) for the [[2027 Baliscan confederal election|2027 elections]], Balisca is divided into 744 (up from 736 in [[2020 Baliscan electoral redistribution|2020]]) commune districts.

Each district represents at least 150,000 people, an entire [[Cantons of Balisca|canton]] or [[Regions of Balisca|region]] (as is the case with [[Cap de Pingüins]]).

